Goals:
-----

 - Develop a treatment of CPU and GPU versions of the same code that:

     1. Allows maintenance of a code base, with a single definition of
     each class that can be used on both CPU and GPU, whenever possible.

     2. Avoiding the need to create parallel sets of nearly identical
     classes (or trivial subclasses) in the Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

     3. Makes efficient use of existing code when possible - avoid the
     need to start over.

     4. Create a manageable build system that allows for maintaining
     object files for several compiled versions of the code. Ideally,
     I'd like to be able to either compile for a single backend or
     maintain versions for several backends simultaneously, at the
     discretion of the user.

     5. Create a manageable system for unit tests, with little or no
     code duplication in the tests.

Comments: Goal 1 has been largely accomplished by the existing system
of class templates defined in namespace Rp, in which "Types" classes
are used as template parameters that encapsulate collections of type
aliases for specialized subclasses. This file proposes a way to
accomplish goal 2 and the remaining other goals. One outcome will be
to avoid the need for distinct Rpc and Rpg namespaces and distinct
src/rpc and src/rpg directories, which will cease to exist.

Discussion
----------

The most basic problem in maintaining CPU and GPU versions of the same
code arises from the need to create CPU and GPU versions of the classes
that store fields that are defined on the nodes of a mesh.  All or almost
all of the arrays that are stored on the GPU in the current version are
fields of this type.  Relevant mathematical operations include things
like binary and in-place (compound) arithmetic operations, exponentiation,
and FFTs.

A design that differentiates behavior for CPUs and GPU-enabled code
could differentiate behavior either at run time (via inheritance and
polymorphic virtual functions) or at compile time (via templates).

A. Run time polymorphism via inheritance and virtual functions:

Strategy: Create an interface / base class for each field class, and define
subclasses designed to use CPU and GPU memory.  Run-time polymorphism must
rely on the use of virtual functions to distinguish behavior, and so requires
that all mathematical operation that are performed on a field must be
implemented by virtual functions declared by a base field class.

Comments:

   - In virtual functions that implement operations for which the output
     is a field, and one or more other fields may be provided as input
     parameters, it is natural to call the member function on the field
     that will hold the output, since there is always one output, but
     there may be any number of inputs.

   - To create generic code, functions that take fields as parameters
     must take references to a base class as parameters. Operations are
     actually only allowed for input and output fields that are defined
     on the same grid and that use the same "backend".  There thus must
     be a run-time check of some sort of label to ensure that all fields
     involved in an operation are compatible in this sense. Because both
     mesh dimensions and backend must be compatible, it is natural to
     create a "layout" structure that encapsulates these two attributes
     and checks them together.

B. Compile time polymorphism via templates.

This is the proposal that is outlined in the remainder of this file. In
this approach, we define class templates that take a template parameter
that uniquely identifies the backend that is in use.

Specifically, to take advantage of analogies with the current design of
class templates in namespace Pscf::Rp, I propose to define a set of
classes that each take two template parameters:

   - The integer dimension of space D=1,2, or 3, and
   - A class parameter T that is uniquely associated with a particular
     backend.

We propose to use a class to identify the backend, rather than some simpler
type of identifier such as an integer, enum or string, because a class can
contain additional information, such as aliases for associated types used
by the related FFT library. The use of a class as a second parameter is
also very similar to the existing system, in which each class template in
namespace Rp takes D and a "Types" class as template parameters.

The class RField that represents real data on a periodic mesh would
thus be declared as "template <int D, int T> class RField" . If we define
a class CPT to represent a Cpu backend and a class CUT to represent
a GPU backend programmed in CUDA, then RField<3,CUT> would be a field
defined on a 3D mesh and allocated in global GPU device memory.

Class templates with these two template parameters would be defined both
for basic Field and FFT classes, and for a variety of higher level
classes that directly or indirectly own such fields.  A class template
Parent<D,T> for a parent class that owns an RField can then have a member
RField<D,T>. In this manner we can create a hierarchy of templates that
own each other, such that all of the templates used in a given executable
would have the same values for both D and T.

Comments:

  - In this case, mathematical operations can be implemented as overloaded
    free functions, for vector operations and reduction operations, or as
    member functions of an associated FFT<D,T> class template, for FFT
    operations. This is what is done currently.  It appears that no changes
    would need to be made to any of the existing mathematical functions.
    Continued use of global functions for vector operationsand reduction
    operations makes it convenient to add new mathematical operations when
    useful without cluttering up the interface of the field classes with a
    growing list of virtual functions.

  - The check that all fields or arrays involved in a mathematical
    operation must use the same backend would be automatically imposed by
    the C++ type system, rather than as run-time-checks: The compiler can
    choose the correct version of overloaded functions for use in CPU or
    GPU code based on the types of the function parameters.

  - I need to think about how to implement unit tests, but it may be
    natural to also re-define these as member function templates that
    take the backend type class T as a template argument. The
    unification of unit tests is not fully fleshed out.

Thoughts:

  - The use of a template parameter to differentiate backends seems
    to be a natural conceptual fit to the way the software is used:
    The intent is that a single backend will always be used throughout
    execution of a given program. The backend for the entire program
    can thus be specified at compile time, as a template parameter
    that is used by every object that needs to differentiate behavior.
    The run-time flexibility promised by a system based on run-time
    polymorphism simply isn't needed, or ever used.

  - I think that the strongest arguments for preferring compile-time
    polymorhpisms are result of some weaknesses of the  C++ language:
    The C++ mechanisms for working with templates in a large code
    base are admittedly clumsier than mechanisms for working with a
    system based on inheritance. The awkwardness of C++ mechanisms
    for working with templates is largely a result of the history of
    the language as an extension of C, the continued use of a text
    preprocessor as the way of importing interface information, and
    lack of an automatic system of avoiding repeated compilation of
    specializations that are used in different translation units.
    When working with templates, one must choose between a design
    in which all function definitions are in the header file or
    use of explicit instantiation declarations and definitions for
    all relevant specializations. PSCF makes heavy use of the
    explicit instantiation to simplify the build process and avoid
    unnecessarily long compile times.  The boilerplate code required
    for explicit instantiation declarations and definitions is,
    however, a real disadvantage of a design based on templates.

  - PSCF now already treats the dimension of space D as a template
    parameter.  The decision to do this was made during development of
    code involving crystallography and space group symmetry, and was
    based on a desire to be able to define small vectors and matrices
    with dimensions given by the dimension of space on the stack, for
    reasons of convenience and efficiency, and to avoid the need to
    store the dimension of space in every class and pass a value of
    D to the constructor of every class.  In retrospect, the focus
    on efficiency was probably misplaced, since operations that involve
    these sorts of small spatial vectors and matrices are never a
    significant fraction of total computation time.  This decision to
    treat D as a template parameter in some classes ended up spreading
    infectiously to to all classes designed specifically for use with
    periodic systems, yielding a design that relies heavily on
    templates in which D is a template parameter.

  - In the current system, the Rp namespace already contains a set of
    classes templates that have two class template parameters analogous
    to the two proposed here. In the process of developing the current
    version, we have already developed methods to avoid some of the
    potential problems of large-template based frameworks in C++ (e.g.,
    potentially long compilation times and mysterious compiler error
    messages) by explicitly instantiating template specializations
    of each class for all allowed values of D=1,2, and 3. This formal
    similarity to the current system will simplify the process of
    conversion.

  - In the current code, for each class template named "Class" in
    the Rpc and Rpg namespaces, there is generally a class template
    Rp::Class<D,T> that defines all or most of the class definition,
    and subclasses Rpc::Class<D> and Rpg::Class<D> that are derived
    from specializations of Rp::Class with different values for the
    Types class. The two subclasses defined in Rpc and Rpg are often
    trivial, with little or no new code, and exist simply to
    segregate names for Cpu and Gpu code into different namespaces.
    In the proposed system, the Rpc and Rpg namespaces would cease
    to exist, and we would work directly with specializations of
    Rp::Class<D,T> with different values for the backend type class
    T.  All classes that are used only by the program or programs
    for real fields with periodic boundary conditions would be
    defined in the Pscf::Rp namespace, using a template parameter
    to distinguish CPU and GPU variants.

  - By getting rid of the Rpc and Rpg namespaces, we would get rid of
    the need for the backend type classes to maintain sets of aliases
    that define generic names that refer to classes defined in the Rpc
    and Rpg namespaces. The definitions of these backend types classes
    would become much smaller, and would contain only a few types
    definitions for types used by the FFT classes and perhaps specialized
    array types (e.g., Device and Host array types used by the CUDA
    code). The remaining simplified types classes could also define a
    few static functions for global operatons that must be implemented
    differently with different backends, such as initialization and
    clean-up of any backend infrastructure.

  - As already noted, the proposed template based proposal would allow
    re-use of all of the existing code for basic mathematical operations,
    without modification. As shown below, it also provides a path to an
    improved design by stepwise changes, without ever breaking the
    code. It appears to me that a change to a fully inheritance based
    design would require a more drastic rewrite.

  - I think it is debatable whether the template-based system proposed
    here would have been the best option if we were designing a new FTS
    package from scratch. If I were starting over, I would see reasonable
    arguments for a system based on inheritance and run time polymorphism.
    Reasonable arguments could also be made for a mixed system in which
    the backend is treated as a template parameter but the dimension D
    of space is passed to each object as an argument of its constructor
    function. The proposed template-based design, with two template
    parameters D and T, is a reasonable alternative that requires
    much less rewriting of the current design. It's main advantage over
    the current system is that it would remove the need for two
    namespaces (Rpc and Rpg) that currently contain many trivial class
    templates, and thereby reduce the amount of duplicate and boilerplate
    code needed to define a system that can use either a CPU or GPU
    backend.

=====================================================================
Build System
------------

- Class templates will all be in Rp namespace, but we need to be able
  to use different compilers for different source files. Users who
  are running on a machine that does not have an NVIDIA GPU should be
  able to compile with a standard C++ compiler (e.g., gcc or clang),
  while CUDA code must be compiled using the NVIDIA nvicc compiler.

- All files associated with a given program will need to be in namespace
  Rp and directory src/rp. This means that files associated with
  specializations for CPU and GPU must have different names.

- Proposal:

    - For each backend, create a makefile variable and corresponding
      C++ preprocessor macro, named PSCF_CPP, PSCF_CUDA, PSCF_HIP, etc.
      that enables use of a particular backend. If the variable
      associated with a backend is defined, then template specializations
      that take the associated backend class as an argument should be
      explicitly instantiated. The PSCF_CUDA macro controls compilation
      of all cuda code, including files in src/pscf, but the PSCF_CPU
      macro is only used to enable template specializations that take
     a backend type class as a template parameter.

    - Modify the options used by the setops script to allow these
      macros to be defined and undefined. Proposed single-letter
      option names:

           -c for PSCF_CPP
           -u for PSCF_CUDA (allow -c for backwards compatibility)
           -i for PSCF_HIP
           -m for PSCF_OMP

      Each option will take an argument 1 to enable code that uses
      the associated backend or 0 to disable that code.

    - Use different file name suffixes and the same base name for
      compilable source files that contain explicit instantiations
      definitions for specializations that must be compiled by
      different compilers, using file name suffixes *.cpp, *.cu, etc.

    - Change the pattern rules in rp for each suffix so as to create 
      object files with different names, such as .o (serial C++) and 
      .ou (cuda) from source files with different suffixes.

    - Change the dependency generation scripts to create object files
      with different suffixes for source files with different suffixes.
      such as .d for serial C++ and .du for cuda. 

    - Note: The dependency generation scripts are used in every 
      namespace level directory that compiles any cuda code, and not
      just in the rp/ namespace. Changing the suffix used for 
      dependency files for CUDA *.cu files would thus require change 
      in all of the makefiles throughout src/ directory of directories
      that contain cuda code. 

    - Each class template should have a header file that contains
      explicit instantiation declarations for all specializations
      that will be built, with blocks of specialization declarations
      enclosed in preprocessor #ifdef .. #endif that test for 
      whether PSCF_CPP PSCF_CUDA, etc. are defined.

    - Mainenance of explicit specialization declarations can be
      simplified by using a preprocessor function macro to generate
      all required declarations for each class template. This could
      be a template named PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE(ClassName) that takes
      the class template name as a parameter. The bottom of each 
      header file in src/rp could have a single line something like

      PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE(Class)

      that the preprocessor expands to

      #ifdef PSCF_CPP
      extern template class Class<1,CPT>;
      extern template class Class<2,CPT>;
      extern template class Class<3,CPT>;
      #endif
      #ifdef PSCF_CUDA
      extern template class Class<1,CUT>;
      extern template class Class<2,CUT>;
      extern template class Class<3,CUT>;
      #endif

    - Files in rp/ that contain a partial specialization of class 
      templates a single backend type T=CPT or T=CUT will use distinct 
      file names for different specializations.  Use name patterns:

           Class_c.h, Class_c.cpp - CPU specialization
           Class_u.h, Class_u.cu  - Cuda specialization

      The suffixes _c and _u will indicate that a file contains an
      explicit partial specialization for a specified backend.
      These suffixes will not be used for header (*.h) and template 
      implementation (*.tpp) files that contain code that applies 
      to more than one specialization.

    - Case 1: Class templates defined in src/rp that do not require 
      explicit specialization for different backends will require 4 
      associated files:

         Class.h    - header
         Class.tpp  - template implementation file
         Class.cpp  - explicit instantiation definitions for CPU
         Class.cu   - explicit instantiation definitions for CUDA

      The Class.h will contain all required explicit instantiation
      declarations, generated by a preprocessor macro. Most class
      templates in directory src/rp will be of this type.

    - Case 2: Class templates defined in src/rp that are defined using a 
      base class ClassBase and 2 explicit specializations of Class will 
      have 7 associated files that contain 3 class definitions:

             Base class template:
                 ClassBase.h
                 ClassBase.tpp
             Serial CPU specialization:
                 Class_c.h
                 Class_c.cpp
             CUDA  GPU specialization:
                 Class_u.h
                 Class_u.u
             Unified header
                 Class.h

      The Class_c.h and Class_u.h header files will each contain
      explicit instantiation declarations for the single relevant
      backend, including those for the required specialization of
      ClassBase and any other required base classes, for D=1, 2,
      and 3.

      Similarly Class_c.cpp and Class_u.cu each contain all only
      explicit instantiation definitions for the single relevant
      backend, for D=1, 2, and 3.

      The unified header can simply conditionally include Class_c.h
      if PSCF_CPP is defined, and conditionally included Class_u.h
      if PSCF_CUDA is defined, as shown below:

         #ifdef PSCF_CPP
         #include Class_c.h
         #endif

         #ifdef PSCF_CUDA
         #include Class_u.h
         #endif

    - Case 3: Class templates defined in src/rp that are defined by 
      defining two partial specializations with no additional base class
      follow a similar pattern but without the two files required to 
      declare and define the base class.  In this case, there would thus
      exist 5 associated files:

             Serial CPU specialization:
                 Class_c.h
                 Class_c.cpp
             CUDA  GPU specialization:
                 Class_u.h
                 Class_u.u
             Unified header
                 Class.h

      Declarations of the primary template <int D, class T> Class can 
      be given within each specialized header file before the definition 
      of the relevant explicit partial specialization.  In this case, 
      no definition exists for the primary template.

    - For files in prdc/field, such as RField:

      Place different files with the same base name in different
      subdirectories prdc/field/cpu and prdc/field/cuda, giving, e.g.,

               prdc/field/cpu/RField.h
               prdc/field/cpu/RField.cpp
               prdc/field/cuda/RField.h
               prdc/field/cuda/RField.cu
               prdc/field/RField.h

      For these classes, we thus use subdirectory names rather than
      suffix codes _c and _u to distinguish files containing explicit
      partial specializations for different backends. The file
      prdc/field/RField.h is a unified header that just conditionally
      includes the relevant headers from field/cpu and field/cuda.

    - The creation of a unified header file for the primary class
      template in cases in which we define explicit specializations
      will simplify the inclusion of header files into other files.
      Specifically, it allows the unified header to be included into
      a *.h or *.tpp file for another class that must allow for use
      of more than one possible argument for the backend type class T.

    - Partial explicit specialization of class templates that uses 
      different backends should be used only when unavoidable.  We should 
      look for tricks that minimize the number of classes for which this
      is necessary.  Code that differentiates behavior by testing a
      enum that identifies the backend could be used in some cases
      as an alternative to explicit partial specialization. To deal
      with situations in which GPU code needs to send data between
      the GPU and CPU memory, while this operation is unnecessary in
      CPU code, templates should be developed that send the data when
      used with device and host array data types used by GPU code but
      that do nothing or simply create a link between two arrays when
      called with corresponding array data types used by CPU code.

    - We should consider converting the DeviceArray and HostDArray
      classes that are currently defined for use with CUDA code into
      templates DeviceArray<T> and HostArray<T>. This would allow
      easier generalization to use of HIP for GPU programming. It
      would also allow for the possibility of defining specializations
      designed for use with CPU code, for which we could define these
      to be trivial subclasses of FftwDRArray. This could allow us to 
      use operations that create an association between a "Device" 
      and "Host" array as analogs for operations that actually send 
      data between the two memory spaces.

=====================================================================
Plan (order of operations)
-----------------------------

Stage I: (non-disruptive preparatory change):

  - Change the few classes in Rp that use several explicit class template
    parameters rather than a Types class into standard two-parameter
    templates that use a  Types class template parameter. This will
    simplify transformation of the role of the Types class from being
    used as a collection of class name aliases to being used as an
    identifier for a backend.

Stage II: (Unify templates that now require explicit specialization)

  - Goal: Try to remove the need to override virtual functions in
    subclassses currently defined in Rpc and Rpg for the few classes
    in which we need different behavior for CPU and GPU.

  - Comment: The main reason for such re-implemented subclasses is the
    need to copy data between host and device in GPU code but not in CPU
    code. We thus need to figure out to create some sort of function
    template that does the write thing for either CPU or GPU code in
    situations in which GPU code requires data transfer.

    These are steps that could be taken either before or after more
    disruptive changes described below:

1)  Make the FftwDArray class from which RField, RFieldDft and CField
    are derived behave like a DRArray, so that it can be referred to by
    other objects of the same type, with reference counting to ensure
    memory safety.  This will make  it is possible to create a safe,
    zero-cost reference to a CPU field to create a structure in CPU code
    analogous to the use of distinct device and host arrays in GPU code:
    In Cpu code a "HostArray" could simply be a reference to a Field
    container, by sharing a pointer, rather than an actual deep copy.
    (Done)

2)  Create template aliases named LocArray, DevArray RLocArray, and
    RDevArray in the Types classes. In the Cuda version, make these
    aliases for FftwDArray or specializations thereof, so that they
    can be used as references. In the Cuda version, makes these aliases
    for HostDRArray and DeviceArray or specializations thereof.

3)  Create overloaded functions sendToHost and sendToDevice in the
    directories that contain the RField and RFieldDft class templates,
    with types that device and host versions of fields. These should do
    an actual copy for Cuda code, but create an reference (copy a
    pointer) for CPU code. Create generic versions of code that require
    copy operations by starting from existing Cuda versions, using a
    using a host array type that is defined by an an alias (e.g.,
    "typename T::HostRField") and calling these overloaded "send"
    functions for data copy operations. For CPU code, the only cost
    will be the creation of an unncessary "dummy" host copy that hols
    a pointer to the actual field, but there will be no deep copy.

4)  Create a few static functions in the Types classes to implement
    one-time initialization operations that are distinct in the CPU and
    GPU versions.  One should be an init() function that does the
    initialization of ThreadArray and ThreadMesh currently done by the
    Cuda System class constructor.  Another one might be a
    initRandomVector function to do the initialization of the vector
    (device) random number generators that is currently done in the
    Simulator constructor.

5)  More generally, work on reducing the number of class templates in
    Rp which require subclasses that need to override virtual functions
    in Rpc and Rpg namespaces to specialize behavior. The less
    inheritance or explicit specialization is used to specialize CPU
    and GPU algorithms in the existing code, the better. This type of
    unification can be done class by class, before or after removal
    of the Rpc and Rpg namespace (stages III and later).

Stage III: (Converting subclasses in Rpc and Rpg to specializations)

    Goal: Get rid of all subclasses defined in the Rpc and Rpg namespace,
    and replace them by specializations of class templates defined in
    the Rp namespace. At the end of the process, entirely remove the
    Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

    The required procedure is different for:

       - trivial subclasses (with no overridden virtual functions)
       - nontrivial subclasses (with overridden virtual functions)

    For each class template named "Class" that is defined in Rp that
    has "trivial" subclasses defiend in Rpc and Rpg:

       - Remove definitions and explicit instantiation of associated
         subclasses Rpc::Class<D> and Rpg::Class<D>, and add explicit
         instantiation of Rp::Class<D, Rpc::Types<D> >.  The Rpc and
         Rpg classes then no longer exist.

       - Throughout the Rp namespace, globally substitute:

             "typename T::Class" => Class<D,T>

       - Throughout the Rpc namespaces (for example), globally substitute:

             "Class<D> => Class<D, Rpc::Types<D> >

       - Change explicit references to classes with specfic values of D
         in unit tests, for example, in rpc/tests:

             "Class<3> => Class<3, Rpc::Types<3> >

       - Remove the associated forward declaration from both Types classes

       - Remove the associated "using" statement from both Types classes

       - Add forward declarations or header includes as required to
         compile any code that is found to have relied on forward
         declarations given in the Types class.

    For each class template in Rp with non-trivial subclasses in Rpc
    and Rpg:

       - Change name of base class template by adding "Base" to name

       - Define an empty primary template for Class without "Base" suffix.
         Declare this near the bottom of the header for the base class
         template.

       - Redefine subclasses currently defined in Rpc and Rpg as
         partial specializations of a primary template Rp::Class<D,T>,
         in which D still a template parameter but an explicit choice
         is made for T in each partial specialization.

       - Go through remaining steps as for a trivial subclass (above)

    At the end of this process, the Types classes should not contain any
    remaining forward declarations or aliases for classes defined in the
    Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

    After conversion of all classes in Rpc and Rpg namespaces into
    specializations of class templates defined in Rp, the only
    remaining classes in these namespaces should be the two Types
    classes.

Stage V (Convert Field and FFT classes to two-parameter templates)

1)  Create partial template specializations templates for RField,
    RFieldDft, CField, FFT etc. in Prdc

    - Create directories prdc/field/cpu and prdc/field/cuda in
      which to place backend-specific field and FFT classes

    - Make explicit partial specializations of RField, RFieldDft,
      CField, FFT, etc. that are just renamed versions of existing
      classes now in Prdc::Cpu and Prdc::Cuda. Place the renamed
      versions in prdc/field/cpu and prdc/field/cuda. Each of these
      partial specializations will still have D as an unspecified
      template parameter, like the existing classes.

      Name changes are:

         Prdc::Cpu::RField<int D>  =>  Prdc::RField<int D, class CPT>
         Prdc::Cuda::RField<int D>  =>  Prdc::RField<int D, class CUT>

      Idea: Use template parameter rather than Cpu and Cuda namespace
      qualifiers to distinguish names of Cpu and Gpu variants of
      analogous field and FFT classes.

    - Note: No change should be necessary to mathematical functions
      defined in the Pscf::VecOp and Pscf::Reduce namespaces. These
      functions operate on DArray or DeviceArray containers, which
      are base classes for the CPU and GPU field containers,
      respectively. These treat fields as simple one-dimensional
      arrays, via inheritance.

2)  Change references to these names throughout the Prdc namespace
    and unit tests to get this code to compile.

3)  In Rp namespace, replace references to aliases such as
    typename T::RFields by template specialization names, such as
    RFields<D,T>, etc.  This will allow us to get rid of the remaining
    aliases in the CPT and CUT
    backend/types classes.

4)  In rpc/ and rpg/ directories, including unit tests, replace direct
    references to, e.g., RField<D> and FFT<D> by RField<D,CPT> and
    FFT<D,CPT>, etc in src/rpc and by RField<D,CUT> and FFT<D,CUT>
    etc.  in src/rpg.

    At this point:

     - All code and unit tests should again compile and run.

     - The Prdc::Cuda and Prdc::Cpu namespaces no longer exist,
       and the prdc/cpu and prdc/cuda directories can be removed.

     - The Rpc and Rpg namespaces will be almost empty, except
       for the Types classes, but the rpc/ and rpg/ directories
       still contain files with explicit instantiation and
       partial specialization definitions for particular backends.

Stage IV (Change location and role of Types classes):

Goal: Move renamed versions of the current Types classes out of the Rpc
and Rpg namespaces. New classes can be named Pscf::CPT (for C++ backend)
and Pscf::CUT (for Cuda backend) and placed in pscf/backends.

1)  Create class template Pscf::CPT by copying Pscf::Rpc::Types<D>
    and create Pscf::CUT by copying Pscf::Rpg::Types<D>. Only a few
    typename aliases should still remain in these classes, referring
    to real and complex types used by the FFT library and to
    specialized array types.  Place these in directory pscf/backends.

2)  Globally substitute:

            Rpc::Types<D> => CPT
            Rpg::Types<D> => CUT

    throughout remaining files in the rpc/ and rpg/ directories.
    Also change file names for the corresponding header file includes.

3)  Define an enumeration class Pscf::BackendId that identifies backends
    and make a value of it a static member of the existing Types classes.
    This would allow code that takes a Types class as a template argument
    to ask explicitly at run time what type of backend is being used, and
    construct logic that can execute different paths for different back
    ends. Place this in pscf/backend.

Note: At this point, The Rpc and Rpg namespaces will thus no longer
exist, but the rpc/ and rpg/ directories will still exist.  The rpc/
and rpg/ directories will still contain header and source files that
contain code for explicit instantiation and (in some cases) partial
specialization definitions for a specific value of T, and thus a
specific backend. For each class that does not require partial
specialization, src/rpc will still contain a header that contains
explicit instantiation declarations for T = CPT and three values of
D and a .cpp file that contains corresponding explicit instantiation
definitions. For classes that still require partial specialization,
the header will also contain a definition of the partial specialization,
with interfaces for overridden virtual functions, and the .cpp file
will contain definitions of all overridden functions. The classes
will, however, all be defined in the Rp namespace.

Stage VI: Change build system, move files to src/rp

1) Modify setopts script so as to set new PSCF_CPU and PSCF_CUDA macros.

2) Add build system files (patterns.mk, sources.mk, makefile) to the rp
directory.

3) Modify pattern rules to allow creation of object files with different
suffixes created from source files with different suffixes. Recommendation:

    *.o   for object files created from *.cpp
    *.ou  for object files created from *.cu

Different suffixes are need to allow the use of the same base name for
Class.cpp and Class.cu and have it generate distinct object files for
CPU and GPU code.

4) Modify scripts used to generate dependency files so as to generate
   files:

    *.d   for dependency files created from *.cpp
    *.du  for dependency files created from *.cu

Different suffixes are need to allow the maintenance of distinct
dependency files for Class.cpp and Class.cu.

5) Modify sources.mk files in src/rp so as to create a list
[directory]_OBJS and [directory]_DEPS such that the _OBJS list
contains all relevant *.o and *.ou object files and the _DEPS
list contains all relevant *.d and *.du files in that directory.

5) Copy specialized header and compilable source files from
   rpc/ and rpg/ into rp/, with _c and _u suffixes for files
   related to explicit specializations for particular backends.

6) Copy *.cpp and *.cu files into src/rp, adding them to the file
   lists in sources.mk as work progresses.

7) Add inclusion of unified header files for class members and base
classes into other *.h files in src/rp as necessary in order to allow
parsing of header files that may now be directly included.

8) Add inclusion of unified header files to *.tpp files in order to
reduce the number of specialized headers that need to be included by
compilable *.cpp and *.cu source files.

Note: During the copying of the *.cpp and *.cu files into src/rp,
separate build systems will exist in rp/, rpc/ and rpg/. Files in
pc/ and rpg/ should initially be left completely unchanged so that
the build systems in these directories remain functional. Inclusions
of header files in src/rpc and src/rpg may be replaced by inclusion
of headers in rp as work progresses, but the build systems should
all remain functional through the process. The rpc/ and rpg/
directories will be removed only after the build system in src/rp
is fully functional and well tested.

Stage VII: Unify and move the unit tests in rp/tests

1) Convert all unit tests to include headers files located in
   src/rp, rather than headers defined in src/rpc and src/rpg.

2) Move unit tests to rp/tests/cpu and rp/tests/cuda

3) Remove rpc/ and rpg/ directories

   Comments:

   1) We should also figure out how to unify the unit tests, but
   have not yet planned for this. As a first tstep this plan simply
   moves the duplicated unit tests into subdirectories of rp/tests,
   with minimal changes to names of header files.

   2) At this point rpc/ and rpg/ directories will no longer exist.

